From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:03:03 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Add an ELF backend for the PDP-11 (elf32-pdp11)

The pdp11 target has only ever had a.out, which has exactly three
sections.  That is what stops -ffunction-sections (the compiler refuses
it outright), makes --gc-sections a no-op, leaves -g with nowhere to
put debug information, and caps an object at 32767 bytes -- all of
which matter rather a lot on a machine with 64K of address space.  No
ELF backend for this architecture exists anywhere: EM_PDP11 is reserved
in the ELF spec and nothing else was ever defined for it.  This is the
first piece of one.

A 16-bit machine in a 32-bit container, the way msp430 already is.  No
GOT, no PLT, no dynamic linking, and ELF_MAXPAGESIZE 1: this target has
one address space, one program in it, and an operating system that
loads a flat image.

Five relocations, numbered by this port since there is no psABI to
follow, and RELA throughout.  Two of them needed a decision:

- A PDP-11 PC-relative operand is reached from the address *after* the
  word holding the displacement.  Rather than a relocation that builds
  that bias in, R_PDP11_PCREL16 is plain S + A - P and the addend
  carries the -2, the same way R_386_PC32 carries -4.

- A 32-bit quantity on this machine is two words, high word first, each
  word little-endian: 0x12345678 is the bytes 34 12 78 56.  That is what
  the assembler's md_number_to_chars writes, so R_PDP11_32 has its own
  relocation function -- bfd_putl32 would silently swap the halves.
  Note that a.out cannot express this relocation at all: its relocation
  entry is a single 16-bit word with a PC-relative flag, a 3-bit type
  and a 12-bit symbol index, and no size field, so `.long symbol` there
  quietly relocates the first two bytes, which on this machine are the
  high half.

Branch displacements get no relocation.  The assembler resolves them
itself and a branch to a symbol it cannot resolve is already an error
rather than a relocation, so none can reach the linker.  If that
changes they can be appended; the numbers are ours.

elf32-pdp11 is selected but not the default for pdp11-*-rt11*: objects
and executables are still a.out, and this only makes the new target
readable and writable (objcopy -O elf32-pdp11) while the rest of the
ELF toolchain is built out.  A pdp11-*-aout build is untouched.

readelf learns the relocation names, so -r prints them rather than
"unrecognized".

No testsuite cases yet: until the assembler can write ELF there is no
way to produce one except by converting an a.out file, and that
conversion has limits of its own (a.out has no section symbols, so an
object whose relocations name a section rather than a symbol cannot be
converted at all).

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

Let a four-byte datum be a four-byte datum in ELF

This machine stores a 32-bit quantity as two words with the high one
first: 0x12345678 is the bytes 34 12 78 56.  The assembler did that for
.long, and R_PDP11_32 had a relocation function of its own to match.

That is the wrong rule for a file.  An ELF header says the file is
little-endian and everything that reads a four-byte field out of it --
the linker, readelf, any DWARF consumer -- takes it at its word, so the
old rule made every four-byte field of the debug information
unreadable.  And it turns out nothing depends on it: a C long here is
two words and gcc emits it as two .word directives in the machine's own
order, never as a .long, so no compiler output and nothing hand-written
in this project uses one.

So in ELF mode .long is a plain little-endian four-byte datum, and
R_PDP11_32 goes back to bfd_elf_generic_reloc -- the special function
and everything around it is gone.  A PDP-11 long is still spelled the
way the compiler already spells it.  a.out keeps the old behaviour.

readelf also learns which pdp11 relocations are the 16- and 32-bit
absolute ones, so it can apply them when dumping the debug sections of
an object file instead of reporting "Missing knowledge of 32-bit reloc
types used in DWARF sections of machine number 65".
